Theoretical Foundations of Moroccan Industrial Sovereignty — The Architecture of Technological Autonomy in Late-Developing Economies

---

1.1 Introduction

The question of what constitutes genuine economic development has occupied scholars, policymakers, and strategists for over a century. Classical development theory celebrated industrialization as the pathway from agrarian poverty to national prosperity. Later, globalization theorists argued that deep integration into world markets — through trade, foreign direct investment, and global value chains — represented the most efficient route to structural transformation. Yet as the twenty-first century unfolds, a more unsettling reality has emerged: a nation may undergo extensive structural transformation, export impressively, and attract foreign capital generously, and still remain fundamentally dependent — structurally incapable of controlling the technological foundations upon which its own economy rests.

This dissertation is concerned precisely with that condition. It investigates the phenomenon of industrial sovereignty deficit: the state in which a nation participates in advanced manufacturing without owning, designing, or controlling the critical technological layers that give such manufacturing its strategic value. The case examined is Morocco — a country that has achieved significant structural transformation in automotive assembly, aerospace subcontracting, phosphate processing, and renewable energy infrastructure — yet which remains, at the level of deep technological architecture, dependent on foreign designs, imported components, externally held intellectual property, and strategic decisions made beyond its borders.

The central argument advanced in this thesis is the following: export volume and industrial output are insufficient measures of economic sovereignty. A country achieves genuine sovereignty not when it produces more, but when it controls what it cannot afford to lose.[^1] This distinction — between production participation and production ownership — forms the theoretical nucleus of the entire work.

The chapter proceeds as follows. Section 1.2 defines industrial sovereignty as a multidimensional construct, positions it against adjacent concepts, and introduces the Strategic Value Capture Equation as a conceptual heuristic. Section 1.3 analyzes the structural ceiling of the assembly economy through the lens of technological capability accumulation theory and its connection to the Middle-Income Trap. Section 1.4 critically engages Global Value Chain theory and its limitations through the sharper analytical framework of Global Production Network theory. Section 1.5 reconceptualizes technological dependency as a national security vulnerability, grounding the analysis in Weaponized Interdependence theory and illustrating concrete chokepoint scenarios. Section 1.6 theorizes the research-manufacturing disconnect as a systemic pathology within Morocco’s National Innovation System. Section 1.7 grounds the proposed policy architecture in Developmental State Theory while acknowledging the contemporary constraints on its application. Section 1.8 critically examines the dual potential of Artificial Intelligence — as a sovereignty accelerant and as a new vector of dependency, theorized as Digital Rentierism. Section 1.9 articulates the falsifiable propositions that guide the empirical investigation. Section 1.10 summarizes the chapter and outlines the dissertation’s structure.

