1.3 Technological Capability Accumulation: Beyond the Assembly-Invention Binary

The theoretical literature on industrial development in late-industrializing nations has long recognized that the transition from basic manufacturing to design-led production is neither sudden nor binary. The foundational work of Sanjaya Lall (1992) established the seminal distinction between production capacity — the physical ability to manufacture goods at scale, which Morocco demonstrably possesses — and technological capability — the deeper capacity to change, adapt, and generate new technologies, which Morocco critically lacks. Lall identified a progressive taxonomy of capability levels through which firms and nations advance: from operational capability (the ability to run existing production systems) to adaptive capability (the ability to modify and improve existing technologies) to innovative capability (the ability to generate original designs and systems). Bell and Pavitt (1992, 1995) extended this framework to demonstrate that technological capability accumulation is path-dependent, requires deliberate institutional investment, and does not occur automatically as a byproduct of production experience.

This taxonomy is theoretically crucial because it dismantles the misleading binary between assembly and invention. Morocco’s industrial trajectory must be understood not as a choice between two fixed positions, but as a migration across micro-layers of capability — from operating foreign-designed systems to sustaining them, then duplicating them, adapting them, improving them, and ultimately generating original designs. At present, Morocco’s industrial base is predominantly concentrated at the operational and partially adaptive layers of this spectrum. The dissertation’s objective is to theorize and map the institutional conditions required to move Morocco systematically toward the innovative capability layer.

This framing connects directly to the Middle-Income Trap — the macroeconomic phenomenon, identified by Gill and Kharas (2007) and extensively elaborated in the development economics literature, in which countries escape low-income status through labor-intensive industrialization but subsequently stagnate before reaching high-income status. Morocco is now confronted with exactly this exposure. As the Carnegie Endowment for International Peace (2024) notes, the country’s export-driven growth model must simultaneously address competition from lower-income countries in labor-intensive industries and from high-income economies in technology-driven sectors, a challenge compounded by its ranking of 111 out of 132 countries in knowledge-intensive employment on the 2023 Global Innovation Index. The Middle-Income Trap is, in essence, the macroeconomic expression of stalled technological capability accumulation. Nations escape it not by producing more of the same goods more cheaply, but by climbing the capability ladder into activities that low-wage competitors cannot replicate and high-income competitors choose not to defend. Industrial sovereignty, in this reading, is the strategic architecture that enables that ascent.

The assembly economy’s structural ceiling manifests across three dimensions. The first is the value capture ceiling. In global value chains, value is distributed asymmetrically along what Shih (1992) conceptualized as the “smile curve,” later formalized empirically by Mudambi (2008): design, branding, proprietary software, and systems integration capture the highest margins; assembly, testing, and basic logistics capture the lowest.[^2] Morocco’s industrial exports are substantial in volume; they are modest in value-added per unit because they are concentrated precisely at the curve’s lowest point.

The second is the innovation ceiling. When a country assembles components designed elsewhere, using machines manufactured elsewhere, governed by software written elsewhere, its engineers accumulate operational skill but not design intelligence. They learn to run production lines but not to conceive them. Over time, this creates what this dissertation terms industrial learned helplessness — a trained incapacity for original design that becomes self-perpetuating because the institutional infrastructure for design-led innovation never develops. Learned helplessness in industrial systems is not irreversible; the psychological literature demonstrates that controllability and successful mastery experiences can undo the cognitive deficit. Analogously, deliberate institutional interventions that give domestic engineers exposure to full design cycles — even on small-scale projects — can begin to restore the design intelligence that the assembly economy suppresses. The point is that such exposure does not occur spontaneously under the assembly model; it must be institutionally constructed.

The third is the geopolitical fragility ceiling, which is theorized in detail in Section 1.5.

Before proceeding, it is worth acknowledging a significant counter-example within Morocco’s own economy: the OCP Group. As a world-leading, state-owned phosphate and fertilizer enterprise, OCP is a design owner with significant intellectual property (over 2,500 patents), integrated industrial ecosystem control, and global market leadership — a partial existence proof that design-led industrial sovereignty is achievable under Moroccan institutional conditions. OCP’s success has been enabled by unique natural endowments, decades of state ownership, and deliberate investment in research infrastructure, notably the Mohammed VI Polytechnic University (UM6P). The phosphate case is not directly replicable in automotive or aerospace, but its institutional learning — the deliberate coupling of research, production, and sovereign ownership — provides a domestic benchmark and a potential source of spillover capability for the broader industrial base.

