1.4 Global Value Chains, Global Production Networks, and the Anatomy of Constrained Upgrading

The dominant paradigm in development economics over the past three decades has been Global Value Chain (GVC) integration. Promoted by institutions including the World Bank, the OECD, and UNCTAD, GVC theory holds that developing nations can accelerate industrialization by specializing in particular segments of internationally fragmented production systems, gradually upgrading their capabilities over time through learning-by-doing and technology spillovers from foreign lead firms.

This framework has produced genuine insights. However, it contains theoretical assumptions that require critical scrutiny through the lens of more recent scholarship, specifically Global Production Network (GPN) theory as developed by Coe, Dicken, Hess, and colleagues (2008). Where GVC analysis tends to focus on value addition and functional upgrading within chains, GPN theory directs analytical attention to territorial embeddedness — the degree to which productive activities become rooted in the institutional and knowledge fabric of host locations — and to power asymmetries — the structural mechanisms through which lead firms exercise control over the governance architecture of production networks in ways that systematically privilege their own strategic interests.

GPN theory helps explain with precision why lead firms such as Renault and Boeing structurally restrict deep knowledge transfers to their Moroccan subsidiaries. These firms have strong incentives to engage in what Humphrey and Schmitz (2002) termed “constrained upgrading”: transferring just enough operational and adaptive capability to ensure quality compliance and supply reliability, while withholding the deeper design intelligence that would enable suppliers to become potential competitors or to exit the network. The governance structures of production networks — supplier qualification systems, design approval processes, proprietary component specifications — are institutional mechanisms that reproduce technological dependency rather than dissolve it.

This analysis should not be interpreted as attributing malice to foreign investors. It is a structural consequence of the rational strategic behavior of network-governing firms. Empirical evidence from developing Asia shows that GVC upgrading is possible, and that economies which successfully upgraded within GVCs expanded by an average of 6 percent annually from 2000 to 2023 (Asian Development Bank, 2024). However, African case studies demonstrate that such upgrading requires deliberate government intervention — acting as shareholder, producer, regulator, and negotiator — and is the exception rather than the rule when institutional conditions are absent (ScienceDirect, 2025). The argument advanced here is not that GVC integration is futile, but that GVC participation absent the four dimensions of industrial sovereignty systematically fails to deliver strategic autonomy. The question is not “GVCs or not,” but “GVCs under whose terms?”

