1.6 The National Innovation System: Diagnosing Morocco’s Institutional Pathology

The research-manufacturing disconnect that characterizes Morocco’s industrial landscape is not reducible to a simple coordination failure between universities and firms. It is better understood, within the theoretical framework of National Innovation Systems (NIS), as a systemic pathology in which the institutional incentive architecture of the entire knowledge economy is misaligned with the demands of technological capability accumulation.

The National Innovation System concept was pioneered in three landmark works: Freeman’s Technology Policy and Economic Performance: Lessons from Japan (1987), Lundvall’s edited volume National Systems of Innovation (1992), and Nelson’s National Innovation Systems: A Comparative Analysis (1993). Freeman defines a NIS as the network of institutions in the public and private sectors whose activities and interactions initiate, import, modify, and diffuse new technologies; Lundvall emphasizes the organizations and institutions involved in searching and exploring, including R&D departments, technological institutes, and universities, as well as all parts of the economic structure affecting learning; Nelson focuses on the set of institutions whose interactions determine the innovative performance of national firms.

Applied to Morocco, this framework reveals multiple layers of systemic dysfunction. At the micro level, domestic firms have insufficient incentive to invest in R&D when cheaper foreign technology is available through licensing and partnership arrangements; the short-term cost calculus consistently favors technology importation over capability building. At the meso level, research institutions and universities operate under academic incentive structures — optimized for publication, citation, and disciplinary prestige — that are fundamentally decoupled from the applied demands of industrial innovation. At the macro level, Morocco’s industrial policy has historically prioritized export performance and foreign investment attraction over the slower, more expensive, and institutionally demanding work of building domestic technological depth.

Lall’s three-pronged framework for analyzing national technological capability identifies the interplay of capabilities, incentives, and institutions as the organizing analytical structure: physical investment, human capital, and technological effort are strongly interlinked, but if physical capital is accumulated without the skills or technology needed to operate it efficiently, national technological capability will not develop adequately; and if formal skills are created but not combined with technological effort, efficiency will not increase dynamically. Morocco has invested significantly in the first two pillars — industrial infrastructure and educational capacity — while the third pillar, systematic technological effort embedded in its production systems, remains critically underdeveloped.

The consequence is what this dissertation terms a decoupled industrial ecosystem: Morocco’s universities produce engineers, but these engineers largely enter foreign firms or public administration rather than domestic deep-tech ventures. Morocco’s industrial clusters generate production experience, but this experience does not feed back into domestic research agendas. Morocco’s foreign partnerships create operational know-how, but this know-how is not systematically captured and codified into sovereign institutional capability. The National Industrial Sovereignty Framework proposed in Chapter 4 is designed specifically to rewire these institutional feedback loops.

