1.8 Artificial Intelligence as Industrial Accelerant: The Double-Edged Sword and the Trap of Digital Rentierism

The emergence of advanced artificial intelligence as a general-purpose technology creates both new possibilities and new dangers for nations in Morocco’s structural position. It is essential to theorize both with equal rigor, resisting the twin temptations of uncritical techno-optimism on the one side and reflexive skepticism on the other.

The potential of AI as an industrial sovereignty accelerant is real and theoretically grounded. AI-driven optimization systems can compress the learning curves of precision manufacturing — enabling domestic firms to achieve quality levels that previously required decades of accumulated engineering experience. AI-assisted design tools can allow engineers in emerging-economy firms to participate in product development at levels of sophistication that would otherwise require far larger R&D establishments. Predictive supply chain systems can identify strategic dependency vulnerabilities before disruption occurs, enabling preventive policy responses. In capability accumulation terms, AI has the potential to accelerate the movement from operational to adaptive capability — and potentially toward the lower levels of innovative capability — in ways that were historically far more time-consuming and capital-intensive.

However, a theoretically rigorous analysis must immediately confront a profound structural irony: AI itself is currently one of the most highly monopolized, centralized technological architectures in modern industrial history. A small number of hyperscale technology corporations — predominantly American and increasingly Chinese — control the foundational model infrastructure, cloud compute capacity, and proprietary training data on which most AI applications depend. If Morocco deploys AI to optimize its industrial systems by routing its production intelligence through foreign proprietary foundational models and cloud architectures, it does not thereby achieve industrial sovereignty. It trades mechanical dependency for what this dissertation terms Digital Rentierism — a new layer of technological neo-colonialism in which the rents of industrial intelligence are extracted by foreign AI platform owners rather than by foreign component manufacturers.[^3] The strategic vulnerability is structurally identical; only its technological medium has changed.

This critical insight generates a specific theoretical requirement for the National Industrial Sovereignty Framework: AI must be conceptualized not as an imported software tool procured from foreign vendors, but as a localized infrastructure requiring domestic fine-tuning, sovereign data ownership, and edge-computing autonomy applied to specific industrial niches where Morocco possesses or can develop genuine data advantages — phosphate chemistry optimization, aerospace metallurgy inspection, automotive process control, and agricultural-industrial interface systems. The distinction between deploying foreign AI and developing sovereign AI capability is the same distinction, at the algorithmic layer, as the distinction between assembling foreign components and designing domestic systems. Morocco must be vigilant against replicating, in the domain of digital intelligence, the same dependency structure it is attempting to escape in the domain of physical manufacturing.

This framing does not negate AI’s potential contribution to Morocco’s industrial sovereignty trajectory. It specifies the conditions under which that contribution genuinely advances sovereignty rather than merely displacing dependency from one technological layer to another. AI is a powerful accelerant within a sovereign industrial strategy; it is a new trap when deployed as a substitute for one.

