1.9 Propositions Guiding the Empirical Investigation

The theoretical architecture constructed in this chapter generates a set of falsifiable propositions that guide the empirical chapters that follow. These propositions operationalize the conceptual framework and provide a transparent chain of reasoning from theory to evidence.

Proposition 1 (Sovereignty-Export Decoupling): Industrial sovereignty, as measured by the composite S_{ind} heuristic, is not significantly correlated with manufacturing export volume in Morocco. High export performance can coexist with low sovereignty when exports are generated predominantly within the assembly model.

Proposition 2 (Design Ownership as Binding Constraint): Domestic design ownership (D) is the binding constraint on Moroccan industrial sovereignty. Improvements in manufacturing depth (M) and supply chain independence (C) alone will not enable Morocco to cross a critical sovereignty threshold unless accompanied by significant progress in design ownership and intellectual property control.

Proposition 3 (Institutional Integration Accelerates Capability Transition): Institutional mechanisms that formally integrate domestic research institutions into the production networks of foreign investors — specifically joint R&D mandates, IP-sharing clauses, and co-location requirements — accelerate the transition from adaptive to innovative capability more effectively than GVC participation alone.

Proposition 4 (Sovereign AI as Enabler, Foreign-Platform AI as Liability): AI adoption increases industrial sovereignty only when deployed on sovereign data infrastructure with domestic model fine-tuning. Foreign-platform-dependent AI adoption deepens Digital Rentierism and reduces the I (IP control) dimension, thereby lowering overall industrial sovereignty.

These propositions are tested in Chapter 2 (empirical mapping of Morocco’s sovereignty deficit), Chapter 4 (institutional design of the NISF), and Chapter 5 (AI’s role in capability building).

