1.10 Chapter Summary and Dissertation Roadmap

This chapter has constructed the theoretical architecture upon which the remainder of this dissertation is built. It has defined industrial sovereignty as a multidimensional construct, differentiated it from adjacent concepts of technological sovereignty, digital sovereignty, and strategic autonomy, and introduced the Strategic Value Capture Equation as a heuristic capturing the multiplicative interdependence of design ownership, manufacturing depth, intellectual property control, and supply chain independence. It has grounded the analysis of Morocco’s technological trajectory in Lall’s capability accumulation framework and Bell and Pavitt’s distinction between production capacity and technological capability, replacing the misleading binary of “assembly versus invention” with a nuanced spectrum of progressive capability layers. It has connected Morocco’s structural position to the Middle-Income Trap literature, establishing that escaping this trap requires precisely the kind of deep technological capability that the assembly economy model cannot generate autonomously.

The chapter has critically engaged GVC theory through the sharper analytical lens of GPN theory, demonstrating that constrained upgrading is not an accident but a structural feature of how lead firms govern production networks. It has reframed technological dependency as a national security vulnerability through Farrell and Newman’s Weaponized Interdependence framework, grounding the abstract risk in a concrete automotive semiconductor chokepoint scenario. It has diagnosed Morocco’s research-manufacturing disconnect as a systemic pathology within its National Innovation System, using the Freeman-Lundvall-Nelson framework to identify the institutional misalignments that must be corrected. It has grounded the proposed policy response in Developmental State Theory, acknowledging the contemporary constraints of WTO provisions and EU trade agreements while specifying the institutional mechanisms that remain available and strategically potent. It has theorized AI as a double-edged instrument that can either accelerate sovereignty building or reproduce dependency as Digital Rentierism, depending entirely on the institutional conditions of its deployment. And it has articulated four falsifiable propositions that guide the empirical investigation.

The chapters that follow build on this foundation in the following sequence. Chapter 2 conducts a detailed empirical analysis of Morocco’s current industrial landscape, mapping its existing capabilities against the four sovereignty dimensions using the operationalized metrics introduced here. Chapter 3 examines comparative cases — South Korea, Taiwan, and Israel — to extract transferable lessons from successful capability transitions in different geopolitical and institutional contexts. Chapter 4 develops the National Industrial Sovereignty Framework in operational detail, specifying the institutional mechanisms, capital allocation priorities, and policy sequencing required for Morocco’s transition. Chapter 5 analyzes the role of sovereign AI systems in accelerating capability building across each strategic sector, including the institutional architecture required to avoid Digital Rentierism. Chapter 6 concludes with the Morocco 2040 Deep Manufacturing Roadmap and the policy and research implications of the study.

The ambition of this work is not merely academic. It is to construct a theoretically rigorous, empirically grounded, and operationally actionable blueprint for Morocco’s transition from a nation that assembles what others design to a nation that designs what the world cannot afford to ignore.

